From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: opencode-port <port@localhost>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 02:48:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] D113 webfetch: HTML that ends inside a tag or a character
 reference converts cleanly
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Upstream: webfetch converts HTML with htmlparser2 8.0.2, which mishandles input that ends inside markup:
- after `</name ` or `<name/` with no `>` it replays the input's last UTF-16 unit as text: `<p>a</p></div foo` gives
  markdown "a\n\no" and text "ao"; `a</b 😀` gives a lone surrogate;
- `<s` and `<t` at the end (script, style and title detection) stay as text: `x<s` gives "xs", while `x<b` gives "x";
- a hex reference without digits at the end loses the text before it: `<p>hello world &#x` gives "";
- an open tag cut off by the end is closed without having been opened, so html-markdown.ts closes the wrong
  frame: `<ul><li>a<b ` gives "- a**", `<p>a<em ` gives "a*".
Fixed (patches/htmlparser2@8.0.2.patch, a patched dependency like upstream's others): a tag the input ends inside
is dropped, as the tokenizer already did for most tag states (and as HTML parsing does at end of file); the
parser no longer closes it; a reference without digits stays text with the text before it ("hello world &#x").
Found by the Rust opencode-html crate's differential harness.
Scenarios: tools-webfetch.test.ts "markup cut off at the end of a page" (new)
Upstream tests: packages/core/test/tool-webfetch.test.ts adds a test

